diff --git a/docs/build/branches.rst b/docs/build/branches.rst
index 3e8bee1..608283c 100644
--- a/docs/build/branches.rst
+++ b/docs/build/branches.rst
@@ -795,3 +795,109 @@ If there's any point to be made here, it's if you are too freely branching, merg
 and labeling, things can get pretty crazy!  Hence the branching system should
 be used carefully and thoughtfully for best results.
 
+
+
+.. _rebase:
+
+Rebasing a Branch onto a New Parent
+-----------------------------------
+
+.. versionadded:: 1.17
+
+The ``alembic merge`` command resolves multiple heads by adding a *new*
+revision that joins the branches together.  Sometimes this is not what we
+want: a long lived feature branch that was created against an older state
+of the mainline should instead be *moved* so that it builds on top of the
+current mainline directly, without an artificial merge revision in between.
+This is what the ``alembic rebase`` command does.
+
+``alembic rebase`` rewrites the ``down_revision`` assignment of an existing
+revision script **in place**, relocating the revision - and with it every
+revision that descends from it - onto a new parent::
+
+    $ alembic rebase d747a8a8879 2a95102259be
+    Rebased d747a8a8879; down_revision is now 2a95102259be (previously 27c6a30d7c24)
+
+Given a tree that branched at ``1975ea83b712``::
+
+    <base> --> 1975 --> ae10 --> 55af --> <head>
+                \
+                 --> 27c6 --> d747 --> <head>
+                      (shoppingcart)
+
+rebasing ``27c6a30d7c24`` onto ``55af2cb1c267`` moves the whole
+``shoppingcart`` branch on top of the mainline, producing a single linear
+history::
+
+    <base> --> 1975 --> ae10 --> 55af --> 27c6 --> d747 --> <head>
+                                                     (shoppingcart)
+
+The command accepts two positional arguments:
+
+* **revision** - the revision script to move.  All identifier forms that
+  other commands accept are supported, including partial revision
+  identifiers and symbolic names such as ``head`` or
+  ``<branchname>@head``.
+
+* **target** - the revision the script should be moved onto.  The special
+  name ``base`` detaches the script from any parent, making it (and its
+  descendants) a new base of the tree::
+
+    $ alembic rebase 27c6a30d7c24 base
+    Rebased 27c6a30d7c24; down_revision is now base (previously 1975ea83b712)
+
+Only the ``down_revision`` assignment of the moved script is modified; the
+``Revises:`` line of the script's docstring header is updated to match.
+Type annotations on the assignment (e.g.
+``down_revision: Union[str, None] = '...'``) are preserved, and the
+revision's own identifier, message, branch labels and dependencies are
+left untouched, as are all other files in the versions directory.
+
+Because rebases are cheap and reversible, the previous ``down_revision``
+value is appended to a ``Rebased from:`` line in the docstring header,
+producing an ordered audit trail of every previous parent that the script
+has ever had::
+
+    """my feature revision
+
+    Revision ID: 27c6a30d7c24
+    Revises: 55af2cb1c267
+    Rebased from: 1975ea83b712
+    Create Date: 2024-05-01 12:00:00.000000
+
+    """
+
+Subsequent rebases extend the list rather than replacing it.  A revision
+that was previously a base is recorded with the literal string ``base``.
+``alembic show`` and ``alembic history --verbose`` display this trail as
+an explicit ``Rebased from:`` line, and both verbose and non-verbose
+listings mark a script that has been rebased with a ``(rebased)`` tag
+alongside the usual ``(head)`` / ``(branchpoint)`` markers.
+
+Since the operation rewrites history in place, a number of guards are
+enforced; the command fails without modifying anything when:
+
+* the revision and the target resolve to the same revision;
+
+* the revision is already based on the requested target;
+
+* the revision is a **merge point**, i.e. it has more than one
+  ``down_revision`` - rebasing a merge would have to discard parents, so
+  it is refused;
+
+* the target is a **descendant** of the revision being moved, which would
+  produce a cycle in the revision graph;
+
+* the script directory is configured as :ref:`sourceless <sourceless>`,
+  since there are no Python source files to rewrite.
+
+Because ``alembic rebase`` edits the revision tree itself, databases that
+were already migrated along the *old* shape of the tree may no longer line
+up with the rewritten history.  The command is therefore best suited for
+branches that have not been deployed yet, or for development workflows
+where databases can be recreated or stamped explicitly with
+``alembic stamp``.
+
+.. seealso::
+
+    :ref:`branches` - resolving branches by adding a merge revision
